To eliminate this channel, we replace the usual unconstrained type of household with “capitalists” (indexed by
), who receive firm profits but do not supply labor. This approach extends to the TANK framework the idea articulated by Broer et al. (2020a) in their critique of the textbook RANK model. The amendment short-circuits the implausible profit income effect on labor supply. The only difference between the U and C types is that the latter are assumed not to participate in the labor market (
) and, consequently, labor income does not enter into their budget constraint.22 As such, cyclical variations in profits no longer determine the effectiveness of fiscal policy through their income effect on labor supply.23

