**Keynesian economics** (  [ /  ˈ  k  eɪ  n  z  i  ə  n  /
](/wiki/Help:IPA/English "Help:IPA/English") [ _ KAYN  -zee-ən _
](/wiki/Help:Pronunciation_respelling_key "Help:Pronunciation respelling key")
; sometimes **Keynesianism** , named after British economist [ John Maynard
Keynes ](/wiki/John_Maynard_Keynes "John Maynard Keynes") ) are the various [
macroeconomic ](/wiki/Macroeconomics "Macroeconomics") theories and [ models
](/wiki/Economic_model "Economic model") of how [ aggregate demand
](/wiki/Aggregate_demand "Aggregate demand") (total spending in the [ economy
](/wiki/Economy "Economy") ) strongly influences [ economic output
](/wiki/Output_\(economics\) "Output \(economics\)") and [ inflation
](/wiki/Inflation "Inflation") .  [1]  In the Keynesian view, aggregate demand
does not necessarily equal the [ productive capacity of the economy
](/wiki/Aggregate_supply "Aggregate supply") . It is influenced by a host of
factors that sometimes behave erratically and impact production, employment,
and [ inflation ](/wiki/Inflation "Inflation") .  [2]

